1. From Scratch to C
In Week 0, you used Scratch, a visual, block-based language. Week 1 transitions to C, a text-based, compiled language. Despite the syntax differences, the same fundamental ideas apply: variables, loops, conditions, and functions.

Key Idea: Everything you did in Scratch can be done in C — just with different syntax.


1.1 Why Learn C?
C is low-level: it teaches how memory and hardware actually work.
C is the foundation of many modern languages (Python, Java, etc.).
Learning C builds disciplined, precise thinking about programming.
CS50 uses C before higher-level languages to build strong fundamentals.

2. Your First C Program — Hello, World!
2.1 The Code
#include <stdio.h>


int main(void)
{
    printf("Hello, World!\n");
    return 0;
}

2.2 Line-by-Line Explanation
Line
What it does
#include <stdio.h>
Includes the Standard Input/Output library — gives you access to printf and other I/O functions.
int main(void)
The entry point of every C program. Execution always begins here. 'int' means it returns an integer; 'void' means no arguments.
{  ...  }
Curly braces define a block of code — the body of main().
printf("Hello, World!\n");
Prints text to the screen. \n is a newline character (moves cursor to next line). The semicolon ends the statement.
return 0;
Returns 0 to the OS, signaling the program exited successfully.


3. Compiling and Running a C Program
C is a compiled language — you must convert source code (.c file) into machine code before running it.

3.1 The Compilation Process
Step 1 — Preprocessing: Handles #include, #define directives.
Step 2 — Compiling: Translates C code to assembly language.
Step 3 — Assembling: Converts assembly to machine code (object file).
Step 4 — Linking: Combines object files and libraries into an executable.

3.2 Using make and clang
$ make hello          # CS50 shorthand — compiles hello.c into ./hello
$ clang -o hello hello.c   # Manual compilation with clang
$ ./hello             # Run the program

Tip: CS50's make command automatically links the cs50 library and common flags. For custom programs, use: clang -o output file.c -lcs50


3.3 Common Compiler Errors
Syntax error: Missing semicolon, mismatched braces.
Undeclared variable: Using a variable before declaring it.
Implicit function declaration: Calling a function without including its header.
Linker error: Function declared but not defined or library not linked.

