6. User Input with CS50 Library
CS50 provides helper functions to safely get user input without dealing with complex C input mechanics.

#include <cs50.h>
#include <stdio.h>


int main(void)
{
    string name = get_string("What is your name? ");
    int age = get_int("How old are you? ");
    printf("Hi, %s! You are %i years old.\n", name, age);
}

6.1 CS50 Input Functions
get_string(prompt) — gets a line of text
get_int(prompt) — gets an integer
get_float(prompt) — gets a float
get_double(prompt) — gets a double
get_long(prompt) — gets a long integer
get_char(prompt) — gets a single character

Important: CS50 functions re-prompt automatically if the user enters invalid input. Outside CS50, use scanf() or fgets() with careful error handling.


7. Operators
7.1 Arithmetic Operators
int a = 10, b = 3;
a + b   // 13  (addition)
a - b   // 7   (subtraction)
a * b   // 30  (multiplication)
a / b   // 3   (integer division — truncates!)
a % b   // 1   (modulo — remainder)

Watch Out: In C, 10 / 3 = 3, NOT 3.33. To get decimals, use: (float)10 / 3 = 3.33. This is called type casting.


7.2 Shorthand Assignment Operators
x += 5;   // same as x = x + 5
x -= 2;   // same as x = x - 2
x *= 3;   // same as x = x * 3
x /= 2;   // same as x = x / 2
x++;      // increment by 1
x--;      // decrement by 1

7.3 Comparison Operators (return true/false)
x == y   // equal to
x != y   // not equal to
x > y    // greater than
x < y    // less than
x >= y   // greater than or equal
x <= y   // less than or equal

7.4 Logical Operators
x && y   // AND — both must be true
x || y   // OR  — at least one must be true
!x       // NOT — inverts true/false

8. Conditional Statements
8.1 if / else if / else
int score = 85;


if (score >= 90)
{
    printf("Grade: A\n");
}
else if (score >= 80)
{
    printf("Grade: B\n");
}
else
{
    printf("Grade: C or below\n");
}

8.2 switch Statement
Use switch when checking one variable against many constant values — cleaner than many else-if chains.

char grade = 'B';


switch (grade)
{
    case 'A':
        printf("Excellent!\n");
        break;
    case 'B':
        printf("Good job!\n");
        break;
    default:
        printf("Keep trying!\n");
}

Remember: Always include break; at the end of each case, or execution will 'fall through' to the next case.


8.3 Ternary Operator (Shorthand if-else)
// condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false
string result = (score >= 60) ? "Pass" : "Fail";

9. Loops
9.1 while Loop
Repeats as long as the condition is true. Use when you don't know the count in advance.

int i = 0;
while (i < 5)
{
    printf("i = %i\n", i);
    i++;
}

9.2 do-while Loop
Executes the body at least once, then checks the condition. Great for input validation.

int n;
do
{
    n = get_int("Enter positive number: ");
}
while (n <= 0);

9.3 for Loop
Best when you know exactly how many times to iterate.

// for (initialization; condition; update)
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
    printf("Row %i\n", i);
}

