9.5 Nested Loops — Mario Example
CS50 Week 1's classic problem: printing a right-aligned pyramid of # symbols.

int height = 4;
for (int i = 1; i <= height; i++)
{
    // Print spaces
    for (int j = 0; j < height - i; j++)
        printf(" ");
    // Print hashes
    for (int k = 0; k < i; k++)
        printf("#");
    printf("\n");
}

Output:
   #
  ##
 ###
####

10. Functions
Functions let you break your program into reusable, named pieces of logic.

10.1 Function Anatomy
return_type function_name(parameter_type parameter_name, ...)
{
    // body
    return value;  // only if return_type != void
}

10.2 Example — Square Function
#include <stdio.h>


// Function prototype (declaration) — tells compiler it exists
int square(int n);


int main(void)
{
    printf("Square of 5 = %i\n", square(5));
}


// Function definition
int square(int n)
{
    return n * n;
}

10.3 void Functions
A void function performs an action but returns no value.

void greet(string name)
{
    printf("Hello, %s!\n", name);
    // no return statement needed
}

10.4 Variable Scope
Local variable: Declared inside a function — only accessible within that function.
Global variable: Declared outside all functions — accessible everywhere (use sparingly).
Variables declared inside { } blocks only exist within those braces.

Best Practice: Avoid global variables when possible. Pass values through function parameters and return values instead.


11. Command-Line Arguments
You can pass arguments to your program when running it from the terminal.

int main(int argc, string argv[])
{
    if (argc == 2)
        printf("Hello, %s!\n", argv[1]);
    else
        printf("Usage: ./hello name\n");
}

11.1 argc and argv Explained
argc — argument count: the number of words typed (including the program name).
argv — argument vector: array of strings. argv[0] is the program name (e.g., "./hello").
argv[1] is the first argument, argv[2] the second, and so on.

Example run:  $ ./hello Alice
argc = 2
argv[0] = "./hello"
argv[1] = "Alice"

12. Memory, Overflow, and Type Casting
12.1 Integer Overflow
Every data type can only hold values within its range. Exceeding the maximum causes overflow — the value wraps around unexpectedly.

// int max is 2,147,483,647
int x = 2147483647;
x = x + 1;
// x is now -2147483648  (overflow!)

12.2 Floating-Point Imprecision
Computers cannot represent all decimal numbers exactly in binary — some values have tiny rounding errors.

printf("%.20f\n", 0.1 + 0.2);
// Output: 0.30000000000000004441  (not exactly 0.3!)

12.3 Type Casting
Cast one type to another explicitly to control arithmetic behavior.

int a = 1, b = 3;
float result = (float) a / b;   // 0.333...
// Without cast: 1 / 3 = 0 (integer division)

