14. Problem Set 1 — Concepts & Tips
Mario (Less / More)
Print a pyramid of # symbols matching a user-specified height.
Use nested for loops: outer for rows, inner for spaces and hashes.
Use do-while to validate input (height must be 1–8).

Credit
Determine if a credit card number is valid using Luhn's Algorithm.
Identify card type (Visa, MasterCard, AmEx) by prefix and length.
Use modulo (%) and integer division (/) to extract individual digits.

Luhn's Algorithm Summary: 1) Multiply every other digit from right by 2, sum the digits of results. 2) Sum the remaining digits. 3) If total % 10 == 0, the card is valid.


15. Code Style and Debugging
15.1 CS50 Style Guide
Indent with 4 spaces (not tabs).
Opening brace { on its own line (Allman style in CS50).
Use meaningful variable names: score not s, counter not c.
Comment your code to explain why, not just what.
Limit line length to ~80 characters for readability.

15.2 Using style50 and check50
$ style50 hello.c        # Check code style
$ check50 cs50/problems/2024/x/hello   # Auto-grade

15.3 Debugging with debug50 / printf
printf debugging: Add temporary printf statements to print variable values.
debug50: CS50's graphical debugger — set breakpoints and inspect variables step by step.
$ debug50 ./hello

Read compiler errors top-to-bottom — fix the first error first; later errors often cascade.
Rubber duck debugging: Explain your code line-by-line out loud — you often find the bug yourself.

16. Week 1 Quick Reference Cheat Sheet
Complete Program Template
#include <cs50.h>
#include <stdio.h>


// Prototype
void do_something(int n);


int main(void)
{
    int n = get_int("Enter a number: ");
    do_something(n);
    return 0;
}


void do_something(int n)
{
    printf("You entered: %i\n", n);
}

Key Syntax to Remember
Every statement ends with a semicolon ;
Strings use double quotes: "hello", characters use single: 'h'
= assigns a value. == compares two values.
int / int = int (truncated). Cast to float for decimal results.
Use #include to access library functions before using them.
Function prototypes must appear before main() calls them.

Final Tip: The CS50 Duck is always there to help — but the real skill is reading compiler errors carefully, thinking through logic step by step, and testing with many inputs, not just the expected ones.


